    "product_id": "5b6bfe83-d4b5-43b9-aeb3-f4635c00a318",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 012330\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n430 PM MST Thu Oct 1 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated 00z Aviation Discussion...\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dry conditions with zero rain chances will persist through at\n  least the weekend and likely much of next week.\n\n- As high pressure builds over the region, a gradual warming trend\n  will bring temperatures at least into a normal range starting\n  this weekend and eventually slightly above normal for next week.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH FRIDAY/... \nMuch drier and quieter conditions are ahead as the low pressure \nsystem that brought the rainy conditions earlier in the week \ncontinues to move eastward and is now centered over Northern \nTexas and the Oklahoma Panhandle. Meanwhile to the region's west a\nlarge ridge is building and is expected to move over the Desert \nSW by late Friday. Until then, today's temperatures will begin to \nclimb, with the western lower deserts already influenced by the \nridge as afternoon highs are in the mid to upper 90s, or near \nnormal. Temperatures in South-central AZ will range in the low to \nmid 90s or just slightly below normal. Starting Friday as the \nridge begins to set up over the Desert SW, temperatures across \nthe entire region will respond to near to 1-2 degrees above normal\nfor early October. During the peak of the heights, late friday \ninto the weekend, the NAEFS climatological percentile is in the \n90th percentile, mostly in western deserts, and along the Colorado\nRiver Valley. Otherwise, the main weather impact for the \nremainder of the workweek will be easterly gusty winds. These \neasterly morning gusts, easily reaching 20-25 mph across southern \nArizona, will supply the area with some weak moisture advection in\nthe low levels. Although this moisture will not be nearly enough \nto bring any rain chances, as the upper atmosphere will still \nremain fairly dry, but it will keep surface dew points in the \n50s.\n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/... \nLittle will change over the weekend and likely even for the bulk\nof next week. The high pressure ridge is heavily favored to remain\nover or at least very close to our region well into next week\nkeeping us under dry air aloft but with a persistent easterly low\nlevel semi-moist flow. Subsidence under the ridge and the dry air\nshould keep a lid on any potential convective chances most days.\nThere are some hints at maybe some convective potential early next\nweek over the highest peaks in eastern Arizona, but as of now the\nhighest PoPs barely breach 10% over the White Mountains.\n\nTemperatures are forecast to rise a bit further during the first\nhalf of next week with the western deserts likely reaching 100-104\ndegrees, while the south-central Arizona lower deserts may get\nclose to 100 degrees. These early October temperatures would range\nfrom 4-8 degrees above normal. Overnight lows will also be at\nleast a few degrees above normal, but generally staying in the\nupper 60s to lower 70s. The most interesting part of the forecast\nwill be the daily easterly breezy conditions due to the expected\nposition of the high over or just to our north and a weak trough\nsetting up to our south. The easterly winds should reach their\npeak over the weekend and during the mid to late morning hours \nwith gusts in some places reaching 30-35 mph. As daytime heating \nincreases and the mixed layer rises, the winds will be more \ndispersed and decrease during the afternoon hours. We should keep \nthese daily easterly mostly morning breezes through at early next\nweek and possibly longer. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 2325Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT:\n\nNo major aviation weather concerns under clear skies can be\nexpected throughout the TAF period. Overall wind directions\nthrough the period will be out of the east, with the exception of\nearly this evening where there may an attempt for a westerly shift\nfor a couple of hours. Otherwise, wind speeds through early Friday\nmorning will remain under 10 kts before gusts upwards of 20-25 kts\noccur by the late/mid morning through the afternoon hours. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\n\nNo aviation weather issues under clear skies can be expected\nthroughout the TAF period. Winds through early Friday morning will\ngenerally be light and variable with periods of calm conditions\nbefore a more easterly direction develops by the mid/late morning\nhours.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nDrier conditions are expected to last through the weekend, with \npersistent clear skies as a high pressure system sets up over the\nregion. Near normal temperatures are expected with the warmest \ntemperatures consistently across the western districts. MinRHs \nwill mostly range from 15-25% over the western districts to 25-35%\nfor the eastern districts with good to excellent overnight \nrecoveries. After light winds today, breezy east winds will set up\ntonight into Friday and persist for several days. Morning wind \ngusts of 20-30 mph will be common starting Friday. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Ryan \nLONG TERM...Kuhlman \nAVIATION...Lojero\nFIRE WEATHER...Kuhlman/Ryan\n",
